---
id: server-routes
title: Server Routes
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server routes are a powerful feature of TanStack Start that allow you to create server-side endpoints in your application and are useful for handling raw HTTP requests, form submissions, user authentication, and much more."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server routes can be defined in your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"./src/routes"},{"type":"text","value":" directory of your project "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"right alongside your TanStack Router routes"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and are automatically handled by the TanStack Start server."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here's what a simple server route looks like:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response('Hello, World!')\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server routes are meant for HTTP endpoints that need to be called from outside your TanStack Start application. If you only need to call server-side logic from within your Start app and want Start to handle serialization for you, use "},{"type":"link","href":"./server-functions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"server functions"}]},{"type":"text","value":" instead."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"server-routes-and-app-routes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server Routes and App Routes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because server routes can be defined in the same directory as your app routes, you can even use the same file for both!"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/hello.tsx\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const body = await request.json()\n        return new Response(JSON.stringify({ message: `Hello, ${body.name}!` }))\n      },\n    },\n  },\n  component: HelloComponent,\n})\n\nfunction HelloComponent() {\n  const [reply, setReply] = useState('')\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <button\n        onClick={() => {\n          fetch('/hello', {\n            method: 'POST',\n            headers: {\n              'Content-Type': 'application/json',\n            },\n            body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'Tanner' }),\n          })\n            .then((res) => res.json())\n            .then((data) => setReply(data.message))\n        }}\n      >\n        Say Hello\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"file-route-conventions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"File Route Conventions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server routes in TanStack Start follow the same file-based routing conventions as TanStack Router. This means that each file in your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"routes"},{"type":"text","value":" directory with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server"},{"type":"text","value":" property in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFileRoute"},{"type":"text","value":" call will be treated as an API route. Here are a few examples:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/routes/users.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" will create an API route at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/users"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/routes/users.index.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" will "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"also"}]},{"type":"text","value":" create an API route at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/users"},{"type":"text","value":" (but will error if duplicate methods are defined)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/routes/users/$id.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" will create an API route at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/users/$id"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/routes/users/$id/posts.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" will create an API route at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/users/$id/posts"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/routes/users.$id.posts.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" will create an API route at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/users/$id/posts"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/routes/api/file/$.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" will create an API route at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/api/file/$"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/routes/my-script[.]js.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" will create an API route at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/my-script.js"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"unique-route-paths","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unique Route Paths"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each route can only have a single handler file associated with it. So, if you have a file named "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"routes/users.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" which'd equal the request path of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/users"},{"type":"text","value":", you cannot have other files that'd also resolve to the same route. For example, the following files would all resolve to the same route and would error:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/routes/users.index.ts"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/routes/users.ts"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/routes/users/index.ts"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"escaped-matching","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Escaped Matching"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Just as with normal routes, server routes can match on escaped characters. For example, a file named "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"routes/users[.]json.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" will create an API route at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/users.json"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"pathless-layout-routes-and-break-out-routes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pathless Layout Routes and Break-out Routes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because of the unified routing system, pathless layout routes and break-out routes are supported for similar functionality around server route middleware."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pathless layout routes can be used to add middleware to a group of routes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Break-out routes can be used to \"break out\" of parent middleware"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"nested-directories-vs-file-names","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nested Directories vs File-names"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the examples above, you may have noticed that the file naming conventions are flexible and allow you to mix and match directories and file names. This is intentional and allows you to organize your Server routes in a way that makes sense for your application. You can read more about this in the "},{"type":"link","href":"/router/latest/docs/framework/react/routing/file-based-routing#s-or-s","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Router File-based Routing Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"handling-server-route-requests","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handling Server Route Requests"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server route requests are handled by Start automatically by default or by Start's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createStartHandler"},{"type":"text","value":" in your custom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/server.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" entry point file."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The start handler is responsible for matching an incoming request to a server route and executing the appropriate middleware and handler."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you need to customize the server handler, you can do so by creating a custom handler and then passing the event to the start handler. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./server-entry-point","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Server Entry Point"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"defining-a-server-route","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Defining a Server Route"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server routes are created by adding a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server"},{"type":"text","value":" property to your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFileRoute"},{"type":"text","value":" call. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server"},{"type":"text","value":" property contains:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"handlers"},{"type":"text","value":" - Either an object mapping HTTP methods to handler functions, or a function that receives "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHandlers"},{"type":"text","value":" for more advanced use cases"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"middleware"},{"type":"text","value":" - Optional route-level middleware array that applies to all handlers"}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response('Hello, World! from ' + request.url)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"defining-server-route-handlers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Defining Server Route Handlers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can define handlers in two ways:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Simple handlers"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Provide handler functions directly in a handlers object"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handlers with middleware"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHandlers"},{"type":"text","value":" function to define handlers with middleware"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"simple-handlers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Simple handlers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For simple use cases, you can provide handler functions directly in a handlers object."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response('Hello, World! from ' + request.url)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"adding-middleware-to-specific-handlers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adding middleware to specific handlers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For more complex use cases, you can add middleware to specific handlers. This requires using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHandlers"},{"type":"text","value":" function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: ({ createHandlers }) =>\n      createHandlers({\n        GET: {\n          middleware: [loggerMiddleware],\n          handler: async ({ request }) => {\n            return new Response('Hello, World! from ' + request.url)\n          },\n        },\n      }),\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"adding-middleware-to-all-handlers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adding middleware to all handlers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can also add middleware that applies to all handlers in a route by using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"middleware"},{"type":"text","value":" property at the server level:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    middleware: [authMiddleware, loggerMiddleware], // Applies to all handlers\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response('Hello, World! from ' + request.url)\n      },\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const body = await request.json()\n        return new Response(`Hello, ${body.name}!`)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"combining-route-level-and-handler-specific-middleware","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Combining route-level and handler-specific middleware"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can combine both approaches - route-level middleware will run first, followed by handler-specific middleware:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    middleware: [authMiddleware], // Runs first for all handlers\n    handlers: ({ createHandlers }) =>\n      createHandlers({\n        GET: async ({ request }) => {\n          return new Response('Hello, World!')\n        },\n        POST: {\n          middleware: [validationMiddleware], // Runs after authMiddleware, only for POST\n          handler: async ({ request }) => {\n            const body = await request.json()\n            return new Response(`Hello, ${body.name}!`)\n          },\n        },\n      }),\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"handler-context","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handler Context"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each HTTP method handler receives an object with the following properties:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"request"},{"type":"text","value":": The incoming request object. You can read more about the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Request"},{"type":"text","value":" object in the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Request","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MDN Web Docs"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"params"},{"type":"text","value":": An object containing the dynamic path parameters of the route. For example, if the route path is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/users/$id"},{"type":"text","value":", and the request is made to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/users/123"},{"type":"text","value":", then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"params"},{"type":"text","value":" will be "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ id: '123' }"},{"type":"text","value":". We'll cover dynamic path parameters and wildcard parameters later in this guide."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":": An object containing the context of the request. This is useful for passing data between middleware."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once you've processed the request, you can return a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Response"},{"type":"text","value":" object or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Promise<Response>"},{"type":"text","value":" or even use any of the helpers from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/react-start"},{"type":"text","value":" to manipulate the response."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"dynamic-path-params","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic Path Params"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server routes support dynamic path parameters in the same way as TanStack Router. For example, a file named "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"routes/users/$id.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" will create an API route at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/users/$id"},{"type":"text","value":" that accepts a dynamic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":" parameter."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/users/$id.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/users/$id')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ params }) => {\n        const { id } = params\n        return new Response(`User ID: ${id}`)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Visit /users/123 to see the response\n// User ID: 123","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can also have multiple dynamic path parameters in a single route. For example, a file named "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"routes/users/$id/posts/$postId.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" will create an API route at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/users/$id/posts/$postId"},{"type":"text","value":" that accepts two dynamic parameters."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/users/$id/posts/$postId.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/users/$id/posts/$postId')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ params }) => {\n        const { id, postId } = params\n        return new Response(`User ID: ${id}, Post ID: ${postId}`)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Visit /users/123/posts/456 to see the response\n// User ID: 123, Post ID: 456","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"wildcard-splat-param","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wildcard/Splat Param"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server routes also support wildcard parameters at the end of the path, which are denoted by a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"$"},{"type":"text","value":" followed by nothing. For example, a file named "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"routes/file/$.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" will create an API route at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/file/$"},{"type":"text","value":" that accepts a wildcard parameter."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/file/$.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/file/$')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ params }) => {\n        const { _splat } = params\n        return new Response(`File: ${_splat}`)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Visit /file/hello.txt to see the response\n// File: hello.txt","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"handling-requests-with-a-body","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handling requests with a body"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To handle POST requests, you can add a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"POST"},{"type":"text","value":" handler to the route object. The handler will receive the request object as the first argument, and you can access the request body using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"request.json()"},{"type":"text","value":" method."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const body = await request.json()\n        return new Response(`Hello, ${body.name}!`)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Send a POST request to /hello with a JSON body like { \"name\": \"Tanner\" }\n// Hello, Tanner!","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This also applies to other HTTP methods like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PUT"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PATCH"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DELETE"},{"type":"text","value":". You can add handlers for these methods in the route object and access the request body using the appropriate method."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It's important to remember that the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"request.json()"},{"type":"text","value":" method returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Promise"},{"type":"text","value":" that resolves to the parsed JSON body of the request. You need to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"await"},{"type":"text","value":" the result to access the body."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is a common pattern for handling POST requests in Server routes/ You can also use other methods like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"request.text()"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"request.formData()"},{"type":"text","value":" to access the body of the request."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"responding-with-json","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Responding with JSON"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When returning JSON using a Response object, this is a common pattern:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response(JSON.stringify({ message: 'Hello, World!' }), {\n          headers: {\n            'Content-Type': 'application/json',\n          },\n        })\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Visit /hello to see the response\n// {\"message\":\"Hello, World!\"}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"using-the-response-json-helper-function","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Response.json"},{"type":"text","value":" helper function"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Or you can use the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Response/json_static","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Response.json"}]},{"type":"text","value":" helper function to automatically set the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Content-Type"},{"type":"text","value":" header to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"application/json"},{"type":"text","value":" and serialize the JSON object for you."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return Response.json({ message: 'Hello, World!' })\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Visit /hello to see the response\n// {\"message\":\"Hello, World!\"}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"responding-with-a-status-code","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Responding with a status code"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can set the status code of the response by passing it as a property of the second argument to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Response"},{"type":"text","value":" constructor"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request, params }) => {\n        const user = await findUser(params.id)\n        if (!user) {\n          return new Response('User not found', {\n            status: 404,\n          })\n        }\n        return Response.json(user)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In this example, we're returning a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"404"},{"type":"text","value":" status code if the user is not found. You can set any valid HTTP status code using this method."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"setting-headers-in-the-response","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Setting headers in the response"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sometimes you may need to set headers in the response. You can do this by passing an object as the second argument to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Response"},{"type":"text","value":" constructor."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response('Hello, World!', {\n          headers: {\n            'Content-Type': 'text/plain',\n          },\n        })\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n// Visit /hello to see the response\n// Hello, World!","lang":"ts"}],"headings":[{"id":"server-routes-and-app-routes","text":"Server Routes and App Routes","level":2},{"id":"file-route-conventions","text":"File Route Conventions","level":2},{"id":"unique-route-paths","text":"Unique Route Paths","level":2},{"id":"escaped-matching","text":"Escaped Matching","level":2},{"id":"pathless-layout-routes-and-break-out-routes","text":"Pathless Layout Routes and Break-out Routes","level":2},{"id":"nested-directories-vs-file-names","text":"Nested Directories vs File-names","level":2},{"id":"handling-server-route-requests","text":"Handling Server Route Requests","level":2},{"id":"defining-a-server-route","text":"Defining a Server Route","level":2},{"id":"defining-server-route-handlers","text":"Defining Server Route Handlers","level":2},{"id":"simple-handlers","text":"Simple handlers","level":3},{"id":"adding-middleware-to-specific-handlers","text":"Adding middleware to specific handlers","level":3},{"id":"adding-middleware-to-all-handlers","text":"Adding middleware to all handlers","level":3},{"id":"combining-route-level-and-handler-specific-middleware","text":"Combining route-level and handler-specific middleware","level":3},{"id":"handler-context","text":"Handler Context","level":2},{"id":"dynamic-path-params","text":"Dynamic Path Params","level":2},{"id":"wildcard-splat-param","text":"Wildcard/Splat Param","level":2},{"id":"handling-requests-with-a-body","text":"Handling requests with a body","level":2},{"id":"responding-with-json","text":"Responding with JSON","level":2},{"id":"using-the-response-json-helper-function","text":"Using the Response.json helper function","level":2},{"id":"responding-with-a-status-code","text":"Responding with a status code","level":2},{"id":"setting-headers-in-the-response","text":"Setting headers in the response","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nServer routes are a powerful feature of TanStack Start that allow you to create server-side endpoints in your application and are useful for handling raw HTTP requests, form submissions, user authentication, and much more.\n\nServer routes can be defined in your `./src/routes` directory of your project **right alongside your TanStack Router routes** and are automatically handled by the TanStack Start server.\n\nHere's what a simple server route looks like:\n\n```ts\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response('Hello, World!')\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Server routes are meant for HTTP endpoints that need to be called from outside your TanStack Start application. If you only need to call server-side logic from within your Start app and want Start to handle serialization for you, use [server functions](./server-functions) instead.\n\n## Server Routes and App Routes\n\nBecause server routes can be defined in the same directory as your app routes, you can even use the same file for both!\n\n```tsx\n// routes/hello.tsx\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const body = await request.json()\n        return new Response(JSON.stringify({ message: `Hello, ${body.name}!` }))\n      },\n    },\n  },\n  component: HelloComponent,\n})\n\nfunction HelloComponent() {\n  const [reply, setReply] = useState('')\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <button\n        onClick={() => {\n          fetch('/hello', {\n            method: 'POST',\n            headers: {\n              'Content-Type': 'application/json',\n            },\n            body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'Tanner' }),\n          })\n            .then((res) => res.json())\n            .then((data) => setReply(data.message))\n        }}\n      >\n        Say Hello\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## File Route Conventions\n\nServer routes in TanStack Start follow the same file-based routing conventions as TanStack Router. This means that each file in your `routes` directory with a `server` property in the `createFileRoute` call will be treated as an API route. Here are a few examples:\n\n- `/routes/users.ts` will create an API route at `/users`\n- `/routes/users.index.ts` will **also** create an API route at `/users` (but will error if duplicate methods are defined)\n- `/routes/users/$id.ts` will create an API route at `/users/$id`\n- `/routes/users/$id/posts.ts` will create an API route at `/users/$id/posts`\n- `/routes/users.$id.posts.ts` will create an API route at `/users/$id/posts`\n- `/routes/api/file/$.ts` will create an API route at `/api/file/$`\n- `/routes/my-script[.]js.ts` will create an API route at `/my-script.js`\n\n## Unique Route Paths\n\nEach route can only have a single handler file associated with it. So, if you have a file named `routes/users.ts` which'd equal the request path of `/users`, you cannot have other files that'd also resolve to the same route. For example, the following files would all resolve to the same route and would error:\n\n- `/routes/users.index.ts`\n- `/routes/users.ts`\n- `/routes/users/index.ts`\n\n## Escaped Matching\n\nJust as with normal routes, server routes can match on escaped characters. For example, a file named `routes/users[.]json.ts` will create an API route at `/users.json`.\n\n## Pathless Layout Routes and Break-out Routes\n\nBecause of the unified routing system, pathless layout routes and break-out routes are supported for similar functionality around server route middleware.\n\n- Pathless layout routes can be used to add middleware to a group of routes\n- Break-out routes can be used to \"break out\" of parent middleware\n\n## Nested Directories vs File-names\n\nIn the examples above, you may have noticed that the file naming conventions are flexible and allow you to mix and match directories and file names. This is intentional and allows you to organize your Server routes in a way that makes sense for your application. You can read more about this in the [TanStack Router File-based Routing Guide](/router/latest/docs/framework/react/routing/file-based-routing#s-or-s).\n\n## Handling Server Route Requests\n\nServer route requests are handled by Start automatically by default or by Start's `createStartHandler` in your custom `src/server.ts` entry point file.\n\nThe start handler is responsible for matching an incoming request to a server route and executing the appropriate middleware and handler.\n\nIf you need to customize the server handler, you can do so by creating a custom handler and then passing the event to the start handler. See [The Server Entry Point](./server-entry-point).\n\n## Defining a Server Route\n\nServer routes are created by adding a `server` property to your `createFileRoute` call. The `server` property contains:\n\n- `handlers` - Either an object mapping HTTP methods to handler functions, or a function that receives `createHandlers` for more advanced use cases\n- `middleware` - Optional route-level middleware array that applies to all handlers\n\n```ts\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response('Hello, World! from ' + request.url)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Defining Server Route Handlers\n\nYou can define handlers in two ways:\n\n- **Simple handlers**: Provide handler functions directly in a handlers object\n- **Handlers with middleware**: Use the `createHandlers` function to define handlers with middleware\n\n### Simple handlers\n\nFor simple use cases, you can provide handler functions directly in a handlers object.\n\n```ts\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response('Hello, World! from ' + request.url)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Adding middleware to specific handlers\n\nFor more complex use cases, you can add middleware to specific handlers. This requires using the `createHandlers` function:\n\n```tsx\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: ({ createHandlers }) =>\n      createHandlers({\n        GET: {\n          middleware: [loggerMiddleware],\n          handler: async ({ request }) => {\n            return new Response('Hello, World! from ' + request.url)\n          },\n        },\n      }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Adding middleware to all handlers\n\nYou can also add middleware that applies to all handlers in a route by using the `middleware` property at the server level:\n\n```tsx\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    middleware: [authMiddleware, loggerMiddleware], // Applies to all handlers\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response('Hello, World! from ' + request.url)\n      },\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const body = await request.json()\n        return new Response(`Hello, ${body.name}!`)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Combining route-level and handler-specific middleware\n\nYou can combine both approaches - route-level middleware will run first, followed by handler-specific middleware:\n\n```tsx\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    middleware: [authMiddleware], // Runs first for all handlers\n    handlers: ({ createHandlers }) =>\n      createHandlers({\n        GET: async ({ request }) => {\n          return new Response('Hello, World!')\n        },\n        POST: {\n          middleware: [validationMiddleware], // Runs after authMiddleware, only for POST\n          handler: async ({ request }) => {\n            const body = await request.json()\n            return new Response(`Hello, ${body.name}!`)\n          },\n        },\n      }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Handler Context\n\nEach HTTP method handler receives an object with the following properties:\n\n- `request`: The incoming request object. You can read more about the `Request` object in the [MDN Web Docs](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Request).\n- `params`: An object containing the dynamic path parameters of the route. For example, if the route path is `/users/$id`, and the request is made to `/users/123`, then `params` will be `{ id: '123' }`. We'll cover dynamic path parameters and wildcard parameters later in this guide.\n- `context`: An object containing the context of the request. This is useful for passing data between middleware.\n\nOnce you've processed the request, you can return a `Response` object or `Promise<Response>` or even use any of the helpers from `@tanstack/react-start` to manipulate the response.\n\n## Dynamic Path Params\n\nServer routes support dynamic path parameters in the same way as TanStack Router. For example, a file named `routes/users/$id.ts` will create an API route at `/users/$id` that accepts a dynamic `id` parameter.\n\n```ts\n// routes/users/$id.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/users/$id')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ params }) => {\n        const { id } = params\n        return new Response(`User ID: ${id}`)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Visit /users/123 to see the response\n// User ID: 123\n```\n\nYou can also have multiple dynamic path parameters in a single route. For example, a file named `routes/users/$id/posts/$postId.ts` will create an API route at `/users/$id/posts/$postId` that accepts two dynamic parameters.\n\n```ts\n// routes/users/$id/posts/$postId.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/users/$id/posts/$postId')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ params }) => {\n        const { id, postId } = params\n        return new Response(`User ID: ${id}, Post ID: ${postId}`)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Visit /users/123/posts/456 to see the response\n// User ID: 123, Post ID: 456\n```\n\n## Wildcard/Splat Param\n\nServer routes also support wildcard parameters at the end of the path, which are denoted by a `$` followed by nothing. For example, a file named `routes/file/$.ts` will create an API route at `/file/$` that accepts a wildcard parameter.\n\n```ts\n// routes/file/$.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/file/$')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ params }) => {\n        const { _splat } = params\n        return new Response(`File: ${_splat}`)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Visit /file/hello.txt to see the response\n// File: hello.txt\n```\n\n## Handling requests with a body\n\nTo handle POST requests, you can add a `POST` handler to the route object. The handler will receive the request object as the first argument, and you can access the request body using the `request.json()` method.\n\n```ts\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const body = await request.json()\n        return new Response(`Hello, ${body.name}!`)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Send a POST request to /hello with a JSON body like { \"name\": \"Tanner\" }\n// Hello, Tanner!\n```\n\nThis also applies to other HTTP methods like `PUT`, `PATCH`, and `DELETE`. You can add handlers for these methods in the route object and access the request body using the appropriate method.\n\nIt's important to remember that the `request.json()` method returns a `Promise` that resolves to the parsed JSON body of the request. You need to `await` the result to access the body.\n\nThis is a common pattern for handling POST requests in Server routes/ You can also use other methods like `request.text()` or `request.formData()` to access the body of the request.\n\n## Responding with JSON\n\nWhen returning JSON using a Response object, this is a common pattern:\n\n```ts\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response(JSON.stringify({ message: 'Hello, World!' }), {\n          headers: {\n            'Content-Type': 'application/json',\n          },\n        })\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Visit /hello to see the response\n// {\"message\":\"Hello, World!\"}\n```\n\n## Using the `Response.json` helper function\n\nOr you can use the [`Response.json`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Response/json_static) helper function to automatically set the `Content-Type` header to `application/json` and serialize the JSON object for you.\n\n```ts\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return Response.json({ message: 'Hello, World!' })\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Visit /hello to see the response\n// {\"message\":\"Hello, World!\"}\n```\n\n## Responding with a status code\n\nYou can set the status code of the response by passing it as a property of the second argument to the `Response` constructor\n\n```ts\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request, params }) => {\n        const user = await findUser(params.id)\n        if (!user) {\n          return new Response('User not found', {\n            status: 404,\n          })\n        }\n        return Response.json(user)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nIn this example, we're returning a `404` status code if the user is not found. You can set any valid HTTP status code using this method.\n\n## Setting headers in the response\n\nSometimes you may need to set headers in the response. You can do this by passing an object as the second argument to the `Response` constructor.\n\n```ts\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response('Hello, World!', {\n          headers: {\n            'Content-Type': 'text/plain',\n          },\n        })\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n// Visit /hello to see the response\n// Hello, World!\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: server-routes\ntitle: Server Routes\n---\n\nServer routes are a powerful feature of TanStack Start that allow you to create server-side endpoints in your application and are useful for handling raw HTTP requests, form submissions, user authentication, and much more.\n\nServer routes can be defined in your `./src/routes` directory of your project **right alongside your TanStack Router routes** and are automatically handled by the TanStack Start server.\n\nHere's what a simple server route looks like:\n\n```ts\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response('Hello, World!')\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Server routes are meant for HTTP endpoints that need to be called from outside your TanStack Start application. If you only need to call server-side logic from within your Start app and want Start to handle serialization for you, use [server functions](./server-functions) instead.\n\n## Server Routes and App Routes\n\nBecause server routes can be defined in the same directory as your app routes, you can even use the same file for both!\n\n```tsx\n// routes/hello.tsx\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const body = await request.json()\n        return new Response(JSON.stringify({ message: `Hello, ${body.name}!` }))\n      },\n    },\n  },\n  component: HelloComponent,\n})\n\nfunction HelloComponent() {\n  const [reply, setReply] = useState('')\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <button\n        onClick={() => {\n          fetch('/hello', {\n            method: 'POST',\n            headers: {\n              'Content-Type': 'application/json',\n            },\n            body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'Tanner' }),\n          })\n            .then((res) => res.json())\n            .then((data) => setReply(data.message))\n        }}\n      >\n        Say Hello\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## File Route Conventions\n\nServer routes in TanStack Start follow the same file-based routing conventions as TanStack Router. This means that each file in your `routes` directory with a `server` property in the `createFileRoute` call will be treated as an API route. Here are a few examples:\n\n- `/routes/users.ts` will create an API route at `/users`\n- `/routes/users.index.ts` will **also** create an API route at `/users` (but will error if duplicate methods are defined)\n- `/routes/users/$id.ts` will create an API route at `/users/$id`\n- `/routes/users/$id/posts.ts` will create an API route at `/users/$id/posts`\n- `/routes/users.$id.posts.ts` will create an API route at `/users/$id/posts`\n- `/routes/api/file/$.ts` will create an API route at `/api/file/$`\n- `/routes/my-script[.]js.ts` will create an API route at `/my-script.js`\n\n## Unique Route Paths\n\nEach route can only have a single handler file associated with it. So, if you have a file named `routes/users.ts` which'd equal the request path of `/users`, you cannot have other files that'd also resolve to the same route. For example, the following files would all resolve to the same route and would error:\n\n- `/routes/users.index.ts`\n- `/routes/users.ts`\n- `/routes/users/index.ts`\n\n## Escaped Matching\n\nJust as with normal routes, server routes can match on escaped characters. For example, a file named `routes/users[.]json.ts` will create an API route at `/users.json`.\n\n## Pathless Layout Routes and Break-out Routes\n\nBecause of the unified routing system, pathless layout routes and break-out routes are supported for similar functionality around server route middleware.\n\n- Pathless layout routes can be used to add middleware to a group of routes\n- Break-out routes can be used to \"break out\" of parent middleware\n\n## Nested Directories vs File-names\n\nIn the examples above, you may have noticed that the file naming conventions are flexible and allow you to mix and match directories and file names. This is intentional and allows you to organize your Server routes in a way that makes sense for your application. You can read more about this in the [TanStack Router File-based Routing Guide](/router/latest/docs/framework/react/routing/file-based-routing#s-or-s).\n\n## Handling Server Route Requests\n\nServer route requests are handled by Start automatically by default or by Start's `createStartHandler` in your custom `src/server.ts` entry point file.\n\nThe start handler is responsible for matching an incoming request to a server route and executing the appropriate middleware and handler.\n\nIf you need to customize the server handler, you can do so by creating a custom handler and then passing the event to the start handler. See [The Server Entry Point](./server-entry-point).\n\n## Defining a Server Route\n\nServer routes are created by adding a `server` property to your `createFileRoute` call. The `server` property contains:\n\n- `handlers` - Either an object mapping HTTP methods to handler functions, or a function that receives `createHandlers` for more advanced use cases\n- `middleware` - Optional route-level middleware array that applies to all handlers\n\n```ts\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response('Hello, World! from ' + request.url)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Defining Server Route Handlers\n\nYou can define handlers in two ways:\n\n- **Simple handlers**: Provide handler functions directly in a handlers object\n- **Handlers with middleware**: Use the `createHandlers` function to define handlers with middleware\n\n### Simple handlers\n\nFor simple use cases, you can provide handler functions directly in a handlers object.\n\n```ts\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response('Hello, World! from ' + request.url)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Adding middleware to specific handlers\n\nFor more complex use cases, you can add middleware to specific handlers. This requires using the `createHandlers` function:\n\n```tsx\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: ({ createHandlers }) =>\n      createHandlers({\n        GET: {\n          middleware: [loggerMiddleware],\n          handler: async ({ request }) => {\n            return new Response('Hello, World! from ' + request.url)\n          },\n        },\n      }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Adding middleware to all handlers\n\nYou can also add middleware that applies to all handlers in a route by using the `middleware` property at the server level:\n\n```tsx\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    middleware: [authMiddleware, loggerMiddleware], // Applies to all handlers\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response('Hello, World! from ' + request.url)\n      },\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const body = await request.json()\n        return new Response(`Hello, ${body.name}!`)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Combining route-level and handler-specific middleware\n\nYou can combine both approaches - route-level middleware will run first, followed by handler-specific middleware:\n\n```tsx\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    middleware: [authMiddleware], // Runs first for all handlers\n    handlers: ({ createHandlers }) =>\n      createHandlers({\n        GET: async ({ request }) => {\n          return new Response('Hello, World!')\n        },\n        POST: {\n          middleware: [validationMiddleware], // Runs after authMiddleware, only for POST\n          handler: async ({ request }) => {\n            const body = await request.json()\n            return new Response(`Hello, ${body.name}!`)\n          },\n        },\n      }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Handler Context\n\nEach HTTP method handler receives an object with the following properties:\n\n- `request`: The incoming request object. You can read more about the `Request` object in the [MDN Web Docs](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Request).\n- `params`: An object containing the dynamic path parameters of the route. For example, if the route path is `/users/$id`, and the request is made to `/users/123`, then `params` will be `{ id: '123' }`. We'll cover dynamic path parameters and wildcard parameters later in this guide.\n- `context`: An object containing the context of the request. This is useful for passing data between middleware.\n\nOnce you've processed the request, you can return a `Response` object or `Promise<Response>` or even use any of the helpers from `@tanstack/react-start` to manipulate the response.\n\n## Dynamic Path Params\n\nServer routes support dynamic path parameters in the same way as TanStack Router. For example, a file named `routes/users/$id.ts` will create an API route at `/users/$id` that accepts a dynamic `id` parameter.\n\n```ts\n// routes/users/$id.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/users/$id')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ params }) => {\n        const { id } = params\n        return new Response(`User ID: ${id}`)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Visit /users/123 to see the response\n// User ID: 123\n```\n\nYou can also have multiple dynamic path parameters in a single route. For example, a file named `routes/users/$id/posts/$postId.ts` will create an API route at `/users/$id/posts/$postId` that accepts two dynamic parameters.\n\n```ts\n// routes/users/$id/posts/$postId.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/users/$id/posts/$postId')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ params }) => {\n        const { id, postId } = params\n        return new Response(`User ID: ${id}, Post ID: ${postId}`)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Visit /users/123/posts/456 to see the response\n// User ID: 123, Post ID: 456\n```\n\n## Wildcard/Splat Param\n\nServer routes also support wildcard parameters at the end of the path, which are denoted by a `$` followed by nothing. For example, a file named `routes/file/$.ts` will create an API route at `/file/$` that accepts a wildcard parameter.\n\n```ts\n// routes/file/$.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/file/$')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ params }) => {\n        const { _splat } = params\n        return new Response(`File: ${_splat}`)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Visit /file/hello.txt to see the response\n// File: hello.txt\n```\n\n## Handling requests with a body\n\nTo handle POST requests, you can add a `POST` handler to the route object. The handler will receive the request object as the first argument, and you can access the request body using the `request.json()` method.\n\n```ts\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const body = await request.json()\n        return new Response(`Hello, ${body.name}!`)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Send a POST request to /hello with a JSON body like { \"name\": \"Tanner\" }\n// Hello, Tanner!\n```\n\nThis also applies to other HTTP methods like `PUT`, `PATCH`, and `DELETE`. You can add handlers for these methods in the route object and access the request body using the appropriate method.\n\nIt's important to remember that the `request.json()` method returns a `Promise` that resolves to the parsed JSON body of the request. You need to `await` the result to access the body.\n\nThis is a common pattern for handling POST requests in Server routes/ You can also use other methods like `request.text()` or `request.formData()` to access the body of the request.\n\n## Responding with JSON\n\nWhen returning JSON using a Response object, this is a common pattern:\n\n```ts\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response(JSON.stringify({ message: 'Hello, World!' }), {\n          headers: {\n            'Content-Type': 'application/json',\n          },\n        })\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Visit /hello to see the response\n// {\"message\":\"Hello, World!\"}\n```\n\n## Using the `Response.json` helper function\n\nOr you can use the [`Response.json`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Response/json_static) helper function to automatically set the `Content-Type` header to `application/json` and serialize the JSON object for you.\n\n```ts\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return Response.json({ message: 'Hello, World!' })\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\n// Visit /hello to see the response\n// {\"message\":\"Hello, World!\"}\n```\n\n## Responding with a status code\n\nYou can set the status code of the response by passing it as a property of the second argument to the `Response` constructor\n\n```ts\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request, params }) => {\n        const user = await findUser(params.id)\n        if (!user) {\n          return new Response('User not found', {\n            status: 404,\n          })\n        }\n        return Response.json(user)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nIn this example, we're returning a `404` status code if the user is not found. You can set any valid HTTP status code using this method.\n\n## Setting headers in the response\n\nSometimes you may need to set headers in the response. You can do this by passing an object as the second argument to the `Response` constructor.\n\n```ts\n// routes/hello.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/hello')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async ({ request }) => {\n        return new Response('Hello, World!', {\n          headers: {\n            'Content-Type': 'text/plain',\n          },\n        })\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n// Visit /hello to see the response\n// Hello, World!\n```\n"}
